WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.532 --> 00:00:16.742
I into a cabin on the wrecked steamboat to see what was going on.

2
00:00:17.983 --> 00:00:22.646
A man was lying on the floor, tied up, and two other men were standing over him.

3
00:00:23.847 --> 00:00:25.929
One had a lantern and the other had a gun.

4
00:00:26.869 --> 00:00:30.132
They were arguing about whether or not to shoot the man on the floor.

5
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.981
Finally they decided that they would just leave him tied up on the wreck.

6
00:00:36.301 --> 00:00:38.822
When it slid off the rock and sank, he would drown.

7
00:00:39.802 --> 00:00:42.883
Meanwhile, they were going to get in their rowboat and row away.

8
00:00:43.904 --> 00:00:45.824
I ran back to Jim, very scared.

9
00:00:46.744 --> 00:00:50.165
I whispered, "Quick, Jim, there are murderers on this boat!

10
00:00:51.226 --> 00:00:54.366
We have to find the rowboat and cut it loose, so they can't get away.

11
00:00:55.447 --> 00:00:59.648
Then we'll get on our raft—" But Jim interrupted me.

12
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.321
"Huck, we don't have a raft!

13
00:01:02.881 --> 00:01:04.701
It broke loose and floated away!"

14
00:01:05.701 --> 00:01:09.302
We were trapped on a wreck with wicked men and no way to get off!

15
00:01:10.323 --> 00:01:14.124
I realized that we had to find that rowboat right away, for ourselves.

16
00:01:15.484 --> 00:01:20.246
Jim and I felt along the sides of the wreck in the dark, until finally we found the

17
00:01:20.266 --> 00:01:25.687
boat. We were about to jump into it when a door opened and the man with the lantern

18
00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:26.567
stepped outside.

19
00:01:27.828 --> 00:01:32.143
We kept still and he didn't see us as he threw a big bag into the boat.

20
00:01:33.964 --> 00:01:39.288
Both men got into the rowboat, and one man said to the other, "All ready—shove off."

21
00:01:40.209 --> 00:01:44.252
Then the second man said, "Wait a minute—did you go through his pockets?"

22
00:01:45.413 --> 00:01:47.535
"No, I didn't," replied the first.

23
00:01:48.576 --> 00:01:53.079
The two men decided to go back on the wreck and see if the tied-up man had any

24
00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:53.980
money they could take.

25
00:01:55.101 --> 00:01:58.684
The minute they went back inside, Jim and I jumped into the rowboat.

26
00:01:59.836 --> 00:02:03.760
I cut the rope with my knife and the river current began to carry us away.

27
00:02:04.821 --> 00:02:07.903
We didn't touch the oars, and we didn't speak or whisper.

28
00:02:09.625 --> 00:02:11.186
We hardly dared to breathe.

29
00:02:12.988 --> 00:02:18.152
When we were about three hundred meters downstream, Jim took the oars and began to row.

30
00:02:19.193 --> 00:02:21.555
I started to worry about the robbers on the wreck.

31
00:02:22.636 --> 00:02:26.500
Even though they were bad men, I didn't want to just leave them to drown.

32
00:02:27.616 --> 00:02:32.300
The storm blew up again, and as the lightning flashed, we searched for our raft in the water.

33
00:02:33.841 --> 00:02:34.982
Finally we found it.

34
00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:38.244
Just then we saw a light on the shore too.

35
00:02:38.264 --> 00:02:42.247
"Jim," I said, "you keep floating on the raft.

36
00:02:43.228 --> 00:02:45.049
Shine a lantern for me so I can find you.

37
00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:47.951
I'm going to take the boat and go to that light.

38
00:02:49.372 --> 00:02:51.033
I want to get help for the men on the wreck."

39
00:02:53.215 --> 00:02:57.954
As I got close to shore, I saw that the light came from a ferryboat sitting on the

40
00:02:58.014 --> 00:03:01.095
water. I found the captain on board, asleep.

41
00:03:02.216 --> 00:03:05.138
I woke him up and told him a sad story that I made up.

42
00:03:05.158 --> 00:03:11.941
"Pap and Ma'am and my sister I began, and then I started to cry.

43
00:03:11.961 --> 00:03:14.903
"Don't cry, now," he said.

44
00:03:14.923 --> 00:03:17.104
"Everything will be all right.

45
00:03:17.444 --> 00:03:18.325
What's the trouble?"

46
00:03:19.085 --> 00:03:23.428
I said that my family had tried to cross the river late at night, but we had lost

47
00:03:23.508 --> 00:03:23.868
our oars.

48
00:03:25.084 --> 00:03:29.026
Floating helplessly down the river, our boat had hit the wrecked steamboat.

49
00:03:30.446 --> 00:03:34.128
We all climbed onto the wreck, but we realized it would soon sink.

50
00:03:35.468 --> 00:03:40.070
I was the only one who could swim, so my dad said I must swim to shore and

51
00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:40.550
get help.

52
00:03:41.751 --> 00:03:45.693
And now here I was, begging the captain to take his boat and help them.

53
00:03:46.993 --> 00:03:51.495
The captain promised he would get his crew right away, and they would take the ferry out to

54
00:03:51.535 --> 00:03:51.915
the wreck.

55
00:03:52.856 --> 00:03:57.539
In the meantime, he directed me to an inn nearby where I could get warm and dry.

56
00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:03.164
But as soon as he left me, I got into the rowboat and rowed out onto the river

57
00:04:03.244 --> 00:04:04.545
to find Jim and the raft.

58
00:04:06.627 --> 00:04:09.529
I felt good about trying to save the gang from drowning.

59
00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:14.213
I only wished there was some way the Widow Douglas could know what I had done.

60
00:04:15.014 --> 00:04:18.977
When I rowed past the wreck again, I saw that it was now mostly underwater.

61
00:04:20.078 --> 00:04:21.840
I called out, but no one answered.

62
00:04:22.928 --> 00:04:25.649
Then I realized that it was too late to save the men.

63
00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:28.050
I felt sad.

64
00:04:28.551 --> 00:04:32.452
It seemed like a long, long time before I saw the light on the raft.

65
00:04:33.453 --> 00:04:36.134
It was almost morning when I finally caught up with Jim.

66
00:04:37.174 --> 00:04:40.976
We found an island, sank the rowboat, and hid the raft on shore.

67
00:04:42.336 --> 00:04:43.977
Then we slept until the afternoon.

68
00:04:46.038 --> 00:04:50.480
When we woke up, we looked at all the things the gang had stolen from the wrecked boat.

69
00:04:51.424 --> 00:04:55.267
We found boots, blankets, clothes, and lots of books.

70
00:04:56.368 --> 00:04:59.571
We spent most of the day lying under the trees, talking.

71
00:05:00.832 --> 00:05:05.596
Then I read to Jim from a book about kings and dukes, and how fancy their clothes were,

72
00:05:06.476 --> 00:05:09.579
and how they called each other "Your Majesty" and "Your Grace."

73
00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:12.141
Jim was very interested.

74
00:05:12.962 --> 00:05:14.563
He asked me how much a king earned.

75
00:05:14.583 --> 00:05:20.348
"Why, they can have a thousand dollars a month, if they want it," I said.

76
00:05:21.772 --> 00:05:24.795
"They can have anything they want—everything belongs to them."

77
00:05:24.815 --> 00:05:27.538
"What do they have to do to get it?"

78
00:05:28.159 --> 00:05:28.659
Jim asked.

79
00:05:28.679 --> 00:05:31.842
"They don't have to do anything," I replied.

80
00:05:31.862 --> 00:05:36.907
"They just sit around—except maybe when there's a war, then they go to the war."
